  "worked_solution": "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we scale $y=u(t)$ by a factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(t).$\n\n- There is no horizontal shift or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(t).$",
